      "writtenOn": "2026-07-30T16:28:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The original design does not talk about `TargetArgs`, I think its a good addition to the design, may be worth updating the design document as well ?",
      "revId": "1ff6ff285134565a50b0ec974c37706e3976190e",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "83d1fe79_2a5445fc",
        "filename": "configurations/fw-update-conditions/schema/schema.json",
        "patchSetId": 55
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001758
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-06T00:30:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "@manojkiran.eda@gmail.com The TargetArgs were added as part of this implementation based on a ask in https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/pldm/+/76704/comment/baa9efc8_3692844d/ . But TBH i think its an over-kill as we are already passing the named args, whats your opinion? -\n1. Shall we just send SAME named args to all the services if service accepts args.\n2. Or Shall we keep the existing method of implementation where args are calculated based on what service needs as specified in configuration file?",
      "parentUuid": "6e609d46_e8c37fd4",
      "revId": "1ff6ff285134565a50b0ec974c37706e3976190e",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c48cee9_1d6470c0",
        "filename": "configurations/fw-update-conditions/schema/schema.json",
        "patchSetId": 55
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000206
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-06T06:16:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think retaining `TargetArgs` is the safer option. Although the arguments are named, not every pre/post condition service necessarily needs or supports the same arguments, and supplying arguments also changes the systemd unit into a templated service invocation. TargetArgs makes each service’s argument contract explicit and avoids coupling all services to every current or future argument. Sending all arguments would only be simpler if we standardize a common ABI and require every condition service to accept and ignore unused keys.",
      "parentUuid": "83d1fe79_2a5445fc",
      "revId": "1ff6ff285134565a50b0ec974c37706e3976190e",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "25af39f1_bd2c6067",
        "filename": "configurations/fw-update-conditions/schema/schema.json",
        "patchSetId": 55
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000206
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-07T04:14:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Me \u0026 @paligill@gmail.com had a chat offline about this, Based on the discussion, the proposed direction is:\n\n- Remove `TargetArgs` for now and have `pldmd` pass all supported named arguments to parameterized services as listing individual arguments for every component unnecessarily duplicates the common argument contract.\n- Identify parameterized services using the standard systemd `@.service` naming convention. Non-parameterized services will receive no arguments.\n- If per-service argument selection is needed later, `TargetArgs` can be added back as an optional schema property without changing the default behavior of passing all arguments.",
      "parentUuid": "6c48cee9_1d6470c0",
      "revId": "1ff6ff285134565a50b0ec974c37706e3976190e",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
